'The Dean Street pub has been the centre of Soho''s bohemian life since the 1940s. Dylan Thomas, Francis Bacon, Lucian Freud, Jeffrey Bernard, and the entire cast of post-war London bohemianism drank here. The rules are still in force: no television, no music, no mobile phones, half-pints only at the bar (the exception to this last rule is a pint of Guinness). The upstairs dining room is genuinely excellent. The downstairs is a reminder that a pub can be a civilisation.',
